/resources/videos/ /ask-the-experts 301

# Syndicated feature with no body of its own. The page ships an EMPTY
# #article-body and no schema, so js/article.js falls through to
# renderNotFound() and real visitors are shown "We couldn't find that resource".
# It is absent from the sitemap and from every internal link, so redirecting it
# is strictly better than serving an error page. Reversible: delete this rule if
# the record ever gets real content or a real external URL.
/articles/parking-today-small-lots/* /resources/ 301

# Vanity path for the Instagram bio link. Instagram renders the raw URL in the
# profile rather than a title, so a tagged link displays as
# "monetize-parking.com/start/?utm_source=instagra…" truncated mid-parameter.
# This keeps the visible link short while the destination still carries the
# campaign, which the in-app browser needs because it strips the referrer.
# 302 rather than 301 on purpose: the bio destination may be repointed later and
# a permanent redirect stays cached in browsers that already followed it.
# YouTube needs no equivalent: it displays the link title and hides the query
# string, so the tagged URL can sit in the channel link as-is.
# Reversible: delete this rule and put the full tagged URL back in the bio.
/ig /start/?utm_source=instagram&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=bio_link 302
